<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Jelgava</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Mitau</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Jelgava, known in German as Mitau, is a city in central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latvia">Latvia</a> on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lielupe">Lielupe</a> river. It grew around a castle built by the Livonian Order in 1265 and was the capital of the Duchy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/courland">Courland</a> and Semigallia from 1578 to 1795, then of the Russian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/governorate">Governorate</a> of Courland. Its baroque palace was built for Duke <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ernst-johann-von-biron">Ernst Johann von Biron</a> by Francesco Bartolomeo Rastrelli.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/jelgava.jpg" alt="Jelgava" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Ivarsveilins · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Jelgava_winter.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Jelgava lies on a fertile plain only 3.5 metres above mean sea level on the right bank of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lielupe">Lielupe</a>, about 41 kilometres south-west of Riga. At high water the plain and sometimes the town are flooded. It is the largest town of the Semigallia region and a railway centre at the junction of over six lines. Until 1917 the city was officially called Mitau; Jelgava is believed to derive from a Livonian word meaning town on the river, while the origin of Mitau is unclear.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Settlement began between the rivers <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lielupe">Lielupe</a> and Driksa in the 10th century. The crusading Livonian Order, under Grand Master Konrad von Mandern, built a castle on the natural island fortification of Pilssala in 1265–1266, and used it to subdue the surrounding <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/livonians">Livonians</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/semigallians">Semigallians</a> by 1290. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuanians">Lithuanians</a> plundered the town in 1345. After the fall of the Livonian Order in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/livonian-war">Livonian War</a> it became a town of the Duchy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/courland">Courland</a>, received city rights in 1573 and became capital of the united duchies of Courland and Semigallia in 1578.</p><p>The duchy was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polish-lithuanian-commonwealth">Polish–Lithuanian Commonwealth</a>, and Swedish invasions subjected Mitau to several sieges, though it grew as a centre of trade and industry. In 1710 Duke Friedrich Wilhelm married <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anna-of-russia">Anna Ioannovna</a>, later Empress of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>, who ruled as duchess from 1711 to 1730. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ernst-johann-von-biron">Ernst Johann von Biron</a> expanded the city's cultural life, building the ducal palace and opening its first public library, and the last duke, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peter-von-biron">Peter von Biron</a>, founded the Academia Petrina in 1775. Russia annexed the city in 1795, in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/third-partition-of-poland">Third Partition of Poland</a>, and the future <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-xviii">Louis XVIII</a> lived in the palace in 1798–1801 and 1804–1807. Rail arrived in 1868, and by 1914 Mitau had over 45,000 inhabitants.</p><p>In 1919 Mitau was a battleground between Bolshevik Red Guards, German paramilitaries and Latvian forces, and after the Latvian victory in November it was renamed Jelgava. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a> occupied it in 1940. German police and Latvian auxiliaries murdered its Jewish inhabitants in mass shootings during the German occupation of 1941–1944, and the main synagogue was burned. Fighting in 1944 destroyed almost 90% of the city, including the Kurland Provincial Museum and Athenaeum. It was rebuilt in Soviet style as part of the Latvian SSR.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Jelgava had 54,694 inhabitants on 1 January 2022 and is home to the Latvia University of Life Sciences and Technologies, which occupies the palace. Designed by Bartolomeo Rastrelli and built between 1738 and 1772, the palace holds the sarcophagi of almost all the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/courland">Courland</a> dukes. Other landmarks include the baroque St Anne's Church, the tower of the destroyed St. Trinity Church and the Academia Petrina.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Jelgava” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Soviet rule after the Second World War brought collectivisation and heavy industrialisation, with factories such as the RAF minibus plant at <b>Jelgava</b>, and large-scale immigration that reduced the Latvian share of the population to about 52 per cent by 1989.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-latvia">Republic of Latvia · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>The duchy's capital was <b>Mitau</b> (Jelgava).</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-courland-and-semigallia">Duchy of Courland and Semigallia</a></li><li><blockquote>After the Third Partition of Poland in 1795, the nobility of the Duchy of Courland and Semigallia submitted to Catherine the Great, and the duchy, with the District of Pilten, became the Governorate of Courland, with its capital at <b>Mitau</b> (Jelgava).</blockquote><a href="/state/governorate-of-courland">Governorate of Courland</a></li></ul></section>
